1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Now let's look at the memory allocation approaches.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
As I already explained, basically there are two different categories.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The first one is the contiguous allocation, and the second one is called non-contiguous allocation.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So under the contiguous allocation category, that means the physical memory assigned to a process is contiguous.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Or in other words, the logical address space of the process remains contiguous in the physical memory.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So under contiguous allocation, we have fixed allocation, fixed partitioning, and dynamic partitioning.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
For non-contiguous allocation, right, so implies by the name.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So that basically means the logical address space of the process is no longer contiguous in the physical memory.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
It's a non-contiguous physical memory is assigned to the process, right.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:13,000
So under this non-contiguous allocation, so we will cover paging and segmentation.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Now let's look at fixed partitioning first, right.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
So for fixed partitioning, so the memory basically is partitioned into regions with fixed boundaries.

13
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
So what does that mean?

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:39,000
So that basically means the physical memory is pre-partitioned into a fixed number of partitions, right.

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
So the number of partitions is fixed, and the size of each partition is also fixed, right.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Then given a process, logical address space, logical process image,

17
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:05,000
then what OS will do is OS will find a partition that's large enough that can accommodate this process image,

18
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
and assign the partition to the process.

19
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So this present image may not occupy the entire partition, so that means part of the partition is not really in use.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
It's wasted, right.

21
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So when a process is created, then OS finds a large enough partition for the process to assign to the process.

22
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Right.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:37,000
And then when a process is terminated, and the partition is assigned to the process, then it will be reclaimed.

24
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
It will become free, right.

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
It will become available, so it can be reassigned to another process.

26
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Right.

27
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
So you can see the problem here.

28
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
So what's the problem here?

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So obviously, so it's not really possible.

30
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
It's not really easy, right, to determine.

31
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So how many partitions we need to have, and what's the size of each partition.

32
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Right.

33
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
All right.

34
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So that's the problem here, right.

35
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
And it's not easy to determine the number of partitions and the size of each partition.

36
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Right.

37
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:28,000
And so because a process is created dynamically, right, and we don't have prior knowledge, right, about the memory requirement of processes.

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Right.

39
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Then the other problem is that a partition will be assigned to a process.

40
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Right.

41
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And the part of partition will be visited.

42
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
It's not really used.

43
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Right.

44
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So this is called internal fragmentation.

45
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Right.

46
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
So this, this tomology fragmentation.

47
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
So I will, again, cover this later on.

48
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Right.

49
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
So that's a fixed partition.

50
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:02,000
So instead of predetermine how many partitions we're going to have, and what are the size of each partition.

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Right.

52
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
So the dynamic partitioning basically is, we try to create the partition dynamically on demand.

53
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Right.

54
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So in this case, so starting with, right, and the entire physical memory is available.

55
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
So we use this terminology hole.

56
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Right.

57
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So it's a hole that refers to a block of available memory.

58
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
So to starting with, to starting with, the entire physical memory is available.

59
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
We have a very large, big hole.

60
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Right.

61
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
So when a process arrives, then what OS will do is, OS will find a large enough hole to assign

62
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
to the process.

63
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Right.

64
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Then when the process terminates, then the OS will reclaim back the memory space assigned

65
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:57,000
to the process.

66
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
So you can imagine.

67
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:07,000
So as a process created and terminated, then the memory will become fragmented.

68
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Right.

69
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
So you have a, you have a holes of a different size.

70
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Right.

71
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Then in order to do the memory allocation using this approach.

72
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
So OS then need to maintain certain information.

73
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Right.

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:33,000
So first of all, the OS need to know, so which part of the memory are currently allocated?

75
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
What are the allocated partitions?

76
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Then the OS also need to know, so which part of memory currently not allocated?

77
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
What are the holes, right, the free partitions?

78
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Now let's look at an example to illustrate how this can work.

79
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Right.

80
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
So, assuming that, right, so this number of processes, we have five processes.

81
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Then we have a corresponding memory requirement for each process.

82
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
So that's the size of the process image.

83
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Right.

84
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Then we also know how long each process may execute.

85
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Right.

86
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:21,000
So for instance, for process P1, so the size of process image is 600K.

87
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Right.

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
It takes 10 time units to complete.

89
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Right.

90
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
So beginning with a single large hole.

91
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Right.

92
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
The physical memory is free.

93
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
So everything is free.

94
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Now at the time zero.

95
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Right.

96
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:46,000
So all these processes are in the job queue, waiting to be loaded into the memory to execute.

97
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Right.

98
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
So recall the state transition diagram.

99
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Right.

100
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So these are the processes in the job queue.

101
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:04,000
When they are loaded into the memory to execute, then there's a state transition, state change.

102
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Right.

103
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,000
For the process.

104
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
So the process then we are changing to the ready state.

105
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Right.

106
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
From the new state change to the ready state and added into the ready queue.

107
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Right.

108
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So now at time zero.

109
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
So these processes are in the job queue.

110
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Right.

111
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And waiting to be loaded into the memory for execution.

112
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,000
So at this time, then we have a sufficient amount of memory to assign to P1, P2 and P3.

113
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So assign P1, P2 and P3.

114
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:46,000
So after assigning memory for these three processes, then the remaining hole is this one.

115
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
260K.

116
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
So 260K is too small.

117
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Right.

118
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
For the memory requirement for P4 and P5.

119
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:01,000
So that means at time zero, then we cannot load P4 and P5.

120
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:08,000
So only P1, P2 and P3 will be able to load into the memory for execution.

121
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
So P1, P2, P3 will be added into the ready queue.

122
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,000
Right.

123
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
The state will change from new to ready.

124
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
So that's at time zero.

125
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Now, so when P4 and P5 can be loaded.

126
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Right.

127
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
So when this can be loaded is untuned.

128
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Until one of these three processes terminates.

129
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:36,000
So when one of the process terminates, then the memory space assigned to the process will be reclaimed.

130
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Right.

131
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,000
So this three processes, P1, P2 and P3.

132
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Right.

133
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
So this is the corresponding execution time for these processes.

134
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
So P2 will terminate first at time five.

135
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:59,000
When P2 terminates at time five, the memory space assigned to P2 will be reclaimed become available.

136
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
We have another hole here.

137
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
So this hole is 1000K.

138
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
So this hole is large enough to assign to P4.

139
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:10,000
Right.

140
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:19,000
So at time five, P4 then will be loaded into the memory to execute and added into the ready queue.

141
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Right.

142
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
So after assign the memory for P4, then the left over hole.

143
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
So this left over hole is 300K.

144
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
So this is too small for P5.

145
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
So we have two holes.

146
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
So this is 300K.

147
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
So 260K.

148
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
So both holes are too small for the last process.

149
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
That's P5, which requires 500K.

150
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
So we cannot load P5 at time five.

151
00:09:47,000 --> 00:10:00,000
We can only load P5 at time when one of these three processes current in the memory terminates.

152
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
So which one will be terminating next?

153
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
So P1 is already executed for five time units.

154
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
When it will terminate at the 10th, require another five time units.

155
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
P4 just loaded, require eight time units to execute.

156
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And P3 already executed five and require another 15 time units to execute.

157
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:35,000
So the next process to terminate, then will be P1 at time 10.

158
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
At time 10, P1 terminates.

159
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Then the memory space allocated to P1 is reclaimed.

160
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
Then we have a hole here.

161
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
So this hole is 600K.

162
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
And the last enough for P5.

163
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:57,000
P5, in this case, can be loaded into the memory for execution at time 10.

164
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:03,000
So after the memory allocation for P5, then we have left our hole.

165
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
So that's 100K.

166
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
So in this case, three holes here.

167
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
So this hole is 100K.

168
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And the second hole is 300K.

169
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
And the last hole is 260K.

170
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Right.

171
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
So that's how this approach works.

172
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Right.

173
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:27,000
So you can see here, right, so as I just mentioned, as the process created and terminated, then the memory space will become fragmented.

174
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Then we have a hole.

175
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Then we have holes.

176
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Right.

177
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:43,000
And when there are multiple holes that are large enough to satisfy a request of a process and then which hole to use.

178
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
So in this case, right, so we have multiple holes.

179
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Right.

180
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
They are all large enough to satisfy a memory requirement of a process.

181
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Now the question is which one to choose.

182
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
So we need a policy.

183
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Right.

184
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:03,000
So this policy is called dynamic storage allocation policy.

185
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
Right.

186
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
So we did the three policies that can be used.

187
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Right.

188
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
The first policy is called first of feet.

189
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:19,000
So that's basically to allocate the first large enough hole to the process.

190
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Right.

191
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:25,000
But remember, remember, recall, the OIS will maintain a list of available holes.

192
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Right.

193
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Then give a memory requirement of a process.

194
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Then in this case, the OIS will search this list.

195
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Right.

196
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:45,000
The first large enough hole that can be used to satisfy the memory requirement of the process and that hole that may be used.

197
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
So that's called first feet.

198
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Now what is the bias feet?

199
00:12:50,000 --> 00:13:00,000
So for the bias feet, so basically OIS will search the list and found the hole that large enough.

200
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:04,000
And that will generate the smallest level over hole.

201
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Right.

202
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:19,000
So basically, so in this case, the OIS will choose the smallest hole in the list that's large enough to satisfy the memory requirement of the process and use that hole.

203
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Right.

204
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
So if you're using that hole, so that means the level over hole will be the smallest.

205
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:44,000
And in contrast to the bias feet, then for the worst feet, then in this case, so the OIS will search the entire list and we found the large, largest hole in the list to satisfy the request.

206
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:54,000
So that's basically means the left over hole will be the largest.

207
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Right.

208
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So WebR3 different dynamic storage allocation policies.

209
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,000
So first feet, bias feet and the worst feet.

210
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,000
So for both bias feet and the worst feet.

211
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:14,000
So that's required to search the entire list of the three available holes.

212
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Right.

213
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:24,000
In order to find out either the smallest, large enough hole or the largest hole in the list.

214
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:56,360
allocated partitions, right?

215
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:58,820
And internal fragmentation,

216
00:14:59,460 --> 00:15:01,360
so that refers to

217
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:04,440
unusable memory space

218
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:08,360
within allocated partitions, right?

219
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:09,740
So for instance,

220
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:12,300
for external fragmentation, right?

221
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:16,060
So we have an external fragmentation here.

222
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:17,480
You see in this case,

223
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:18,940
we have three holes,

224
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:21,360
but all these holes are too small,

225
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:22,900
maybe too small, right?

226
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:24,820
To be useful, right?

227
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:26,020
So in this case,

228
00:15:26,360 --> 00:15:28,640
the holes here is in between

229
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:30,420
allocated memory partitions.

230
00:15:30,700 --> 00:15:33,760
It's outside allocated memory partition.

231
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,340
So this is called

232
00:15:35,340 --> 00:15:38,420
external fragmentation, right?

233
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:39,860
And for internal,

234
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,520
so this happens here, right?

235
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:43,580
So recall,

236
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:46,360
so we assign a partition

237
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:48,060
to a process,

238
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:49,580
but that present image

239
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:52,340
may not occupy the entire partition.

240
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:54,760
then part of this

241
00:15:54,760 --> 00:15:56,820
is not really in use.

242
00:15:56,900 --> 00:15:58,180
It's wasted, right?

243
00:15:58,460 --> 00:15:59,500
So in this case,

244
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:01,500
so this wastage happening

245
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:04,320
inside an allocated partition,

246
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:05,900
so this refers to

247
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:09,080
internal fragmentation, right?

248
00:16:09,080 --> 00:16:14,200
So there are two different types of fragmentation, right?

249
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:15,140
So in particular,

250
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:19,220
to deal with this external fragmentation, right?

251
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,080
So the OS commonly

252
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:26,060
will use this compaction strategy, right?

253
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:27,820
So for memory compaction,

254
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:30,400
so that basically refers to

255
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:32,780
shuffle memory contents, right?

256
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:35,540
In order to place,

257
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:38,480
merge smaller holes together

258
00:16:38,480 --> 00:16:42,120
to form a larger hole, right?

259
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:45,020
Now let's look at example, right?

